  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nQuiet conditions remain in place with ridging and surface high \npressure in control to bring near average temperatures today. On \nWednesday, a weak surface low will be found to the southeast. Mid \nlevel vorticity will be found alongside it in the afternoon as a weak\nupper low tracks north through eastern Kentucky. This will bring\nshowers and some thunderstorms, generally expected over the se CWA\nbut more likely in the eastern Scioto River Valley. Adjusted pops to\nbe capped at chance, which was a quite minor change. \n\nBy Thursday, a larger trough to our west will move into the area and\ndrag a long, trailing cold front across the Great Lakes region. This\nfrontal boundary looks to stall out just west of our CWA, providing \nthe focus for showers and thunderstorms from Thursday through the \nweekend. Details still need to be worked out regarding how things \nwill progress, but based on the orientation of the front and large \nscale flow, training thunderstorms could introduce a flooding threat.",
